257
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:24.060
Randy said, luckily, the Michael Scott Paper Company storyline was such that Steve generally

258
00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:28.520
had a couple days off each week for when we would shoot up at Dunder Mifflin.

259
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.240
And so he immersed himself in prep for those two days.

260
00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:33.580
But it's true.

261
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.840
I would notice there would be this van waiting outside at lunch and they would go scout a

262
00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.380
location for a half hour.

263
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:41.860
Yeah.

264
00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.960
And then he'd come back and jump into scenes.

265
00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:51.000
Well, I also spoke with Randall Einhorn, our cinematographer, who said he remembers Steve

266
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:57.140
was very focused on characters and actor performance as a director.

267
00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:00.160
So I guess this will come up in the note that he gave you, Angela.

268
00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:00.600
Yeah.

269
00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.820
But he said he was just so respectful.

270
00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.080
He was so devoid of any ego.

271
00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:08.840
And that's how I remember working with Steve.

272
00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.640
He just made you better naturally.

273
00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.880
He's just someone that makes everything better.

274
00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:15.800
Yeah.

275
00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.680
You know, you know those people.

276
00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:20.900
You're like, gosh, I feel so great whenever I'm around them.

277
00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:30.520
I took this leadership seminar once and they said that a great leader is a person where like

278
00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:38.100
you leave your interaction with that person and you feel more confident and better and

279
00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:44.240
you notice yourself more, that that is your goal if you're ever in a leadership position.

280
00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:48.320
Not for you to show off how interesting and powerful you are.

281
00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:48.560
Right.

282
00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.420
But to try to empower other people.

283
00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:52.960
Well, that's definitely Steve.

284
00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:54.440
That's definitely Steve.

285
00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:55.760
Guys, yeah.

286
00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.040
I'm that person who went to leadership seminars on the weekends.

287
00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.800
I wasn't going to say anything.

288
00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:01.380
I'm that gal.

289
00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:07.860
I wasn't going to say anything, but as you said it, I thought, oh, I would have just loved

290
00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:08.940
to have known Jenna then.

291
00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:11.700
I saw this thing someone posted on Instagram.

292
00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.120
Jenna, it made me laugh so hard.

293
00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:18.100
I'm going to paraphrase it, but it said something like, everyone in life deserves the person they

294
00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.700
cannot sit next to in a serious situation.

295
00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:27.680
And I just think about if I had taken that leadership class with you, we wouldn't have been

296
00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:28.840
able to sit next to each other.

297
00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:34.040
Listen, that leadership class was great because I had a really hard time finishing things.

298
00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:37.540
I would start projects all the time.

299
00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:43.620
Scripts, a one-woman show, I don't even know what, even a home project.

300
00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:49.920
I was very good at having ideas and doing the first three steps, but I wasn't a finisher.

301
00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:55.200
And this leadership seminar was a little bit focused on finishing what you start.

302
00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:58.200
I tell you what, I finish what I start now.

303
00:13:58.660 --> 00:13:59.440
That's so good.

304
00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:00.600
I'm all about it.

305
00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:05.000
I'm like, there's got to be experts out there who can teach me skills.

306
00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:08.240
How was I supposed to be born knowing how to do everything?

307
00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:09.920
I'll go to therapy.

308
00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:11.600
I'll go to your leadership seminar.

309
00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:12.520
Sign me up.

310
00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:13.840
All right.

311
00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:17.460
Fast fact number two is called awards.

312
00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:24.360
Because our writer, Charlie Grandy, was nominated for a Writer's Guild Award for Best Screenplay

313
00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:26.640
of an Episodic Comedy for this script.

314
00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:35.220
And Steve Carell used this episode as his submission for the Emmys for Outstanding Lead Actor in a

315
00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:35.980
Comedy Series.

316
00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.960
Well, I know Steve didn't win, which remember I was so mad about.

317
00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.820
I think I said a profanity and there was a camera on us.

318
00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Steve never won an Emmy for his portrayal of Michael Scott.

319
00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.480
How is that sentence a thing I can say?

320
00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:49.100
I don't know.

321
00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.160
Wait, did Charlie win?

322
00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:51.360
Charlie didn't win either.

323
00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:52.420
Well, son of a...

324
00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:53.020
I know.

325
00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:54.480
You know, it's crazy.

326
00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:58.020
That got me thinking about how you have to submit yourself for awards.

327
00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.260
I feel like that's something not a lot of people know.

328
00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:01.640
We did.

329
00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:03.400
Yeah, and it costs money.

330
00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:04.020
Mm-hmm.

331
00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:07.060
Sometimes the network will pay for it.

332
00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:07.380
Right.

333
00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:14.360
But I think as an actor, you pick one or two episodes that you think best represent your

334
00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:14.920
performance.

335
00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.960
You also pick your category.

336
00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:22.140
You can decide if you want to be submitted for lead or supporting.

337
00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:22.940
I know.

338
00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:28.440
I was very excited because season four of The Office, there was an article that said I was

339
00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:34.560
a dark horse for a nomination for supporting actress in a comedy because I had that whole

340
00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.560
great arc with sprinkles and all of that.

341
00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:38.020
Yes.

342
00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.540
And I was so excited.

343
00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:41.240
That was enough for me.

344
00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:47.540
I mean, I realized that some people were able to have sort of a big machine behind them campaigning,

345
00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:52.820
but just the fact that this one person saw my performance, I cut out the magazine.

346
00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:53.540
I saved it.

347
00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.180
I totally get that, Ange.

348
00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:58.620
I was only nominated the one year for season two.

349
00:15:58.840 --> 00:15:59.140
Yeah.

350
00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:03.640
And occasionally, though, my name would come up on those lists.

351
00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.080
Yes, it's so exciting.

352
00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:05.460
Yeah.

353
00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.080
What's your fast fact number three?

354
00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.800
Fast fact number three is called Time to Make the Donuts.

355
00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:11.940
Do I get a donut?

356
00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:13.260
Did you make donuts?

357
00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:15.280
No, I wish I had, though.

358
00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:16.400
I know you love a donut.

359
00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:17.020
I do.

360
00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:18.060
I should have known.

361
00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:21.000
I can't say donut and not provide you with a donut.

362
00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.700
You literally said it's time to make the donuts.

363
00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:24.900
Oh, no.

364
00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:27.060
This is not a Scotch and Splenda.

365
00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:27.680
I know.

366
00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.360
You got me thinking it's a Scotch and Splenda moment.

367
00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:36.940
No, this is a little mini deep dive that I found incredibly charming based on the fact

368
00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:39.880
that Michael says it's time to make the donuts.

369
00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:41.380
Oh, the expression.

370
00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:42.240
Yes.

371
00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.440
Do you remember that commercial?

372
00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:46.500
It was from the 80s.

373
00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:52.440
It actually ran from 1981 all the way to 1997, and it was for Dunkin' Donuts.

374
00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:54.060
Do you remember it?

375
00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:56.320
Well, I was overseas until 84.

376
00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:57.320
Okay.

377
00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:01.060
This, by the way, is a thing my husband loves to tease me about.

378
00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:05.380
He said, Ange, you can't say you were in Indonesia for everything, okay?

379
00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:09.280
But there's some pop culture that's lost on you.

380
00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:09.760
Yes.

381
00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:10.640
Okay.

382
00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:12.480
Well, I grew up without television.

383
00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:13.240
Isn't that crazy?

384
00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:14.820
Until I was a teenager.

385
00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:15.840
Wow.

386
00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:16.540
I know.

387
00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:18.740
And then you wound up on television.

388
00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:21.000
And then I was obsessed with television.

389
00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:21.820
I bet.

390
00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:26.000
It's like the kid that, like, their parents don't let them have any sweets, and then they

391
00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:27.660
come to your house and they raid your pantry.

392
00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:28.340
Yeah.

393
00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.620
I became, like, obsessed with television.

394
00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:32.300
Well, let me bring you up to speed.

395
00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:32.640
Okay.

396
00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:40.460
Fred the Baker was a character played by actor Michael Vail in these Dunkin' Donut commercials.

397
00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:41.040
Okay.

398
00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:44.380
And he was known for his catchphrase, time to make the donuts.

399
00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:51.560
So it would just be him, like, all day coming in and out of his door saying, time to make

400
00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:52.020
the donuts.

401
00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.080
I actually have an audio clip of it.

402
00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.120
Oh, I need to hear it.

403
00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.500
Time to make the donuts.

404
00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.720
Dunkin' Donuts are always fresh.

405
00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:01.860
I made the donuts.

406
00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.860
We make them at least twice every day.

407
00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:05.000
Time to make the donuts.

408
00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.240
Not a few kinds, like supermarkets.

409
00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:09.480
Hey, the donuts.

410
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.240
Time to make the donuts.

411
00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:12.780
But up to 52 varieties.

412
00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:13.720
The donuts.

413
00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:18.340
Time to make the donuts.

414
00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.180
I made the donuts.

415
00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.320
Dunkin' Donuts, up to 52 varieties, fresh day and night.

416
00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:24.940
No supermarket can say that.

417
00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:27.500
So Fred the Baker had to make all those donuts, lady.

418
00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:30.160
I have so many questions, because I could only hear it and not see it.

419
00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:31.700
It sounds like he's getting really tired.

420
00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:32.820
He's tired?

421
00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:34.880
He's making donuts in the rain.

422
00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:36.600
Time to make the donuts.

423
00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.320
Because he's always making the donuts.

424
00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:45.100
Well, this commercial was named one of the five best commercials of the 80s by the Television

425
00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.740
Bureau of Advertising.

426
00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:53.580
Fred the Baker was so popular that when Dunkin' Donuts decided to finally retire this character,

427
00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:58.160
they surveyed customers to see how they might react.

428
00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:08.320
Well, customers said they would let Fred retire, but only if, like, Fred the character was treated like an honored friend and employee.

429
00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:19.560
So, Dunkin' Donuts created an official retirement celebration for him, which included a parade in Boston and a free donut day.

430
00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.360
They served over 6 million customers.

431
00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:25.540
It was September 22nd, 1997.

432
00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:26.800
Wow.

433
00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:28.360
But listen to this.

434
00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:42.380
After the actor who played Fred the Baker after Michael Vail died in December of 2005, Dunkin' Donuts ran a commercial celebrating him that included, like, an in-memory-of.

435
00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:54.600
And today, the catchphrase, time to make the donuts, is printed on the side of all Dunkin' Donut boxes in memory of Michael and his character of Fred the Baker.

436
00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:55.400
Aww.

437
00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:59.880
So, if you get a box of Dunkin' Donuts and you see that on the side, that's why.

438
00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:01.060
That's so sweet.

439
00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:05.540
You know, I think Dunkin' Donuts was more of an East Coast chain for a long time.

440
00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:05.980
Mm-hmm.

441
00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:12.060
Because I know whenever I flew to New York, I got very excited because there was a Dunkin' Donuts by baggage claim.

442
00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:15.440
And I would always go get a donut while I waited for my suitcase.

443
00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:19.240
Yeah, we had Dunkin' Donuts in St. Louis growing up.

444
00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.140
And my dad would go every Sunday.

445
00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:23.920
And I always wanted the strawberry iced.

446
00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:25.100
I love that one.

447
00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:25.860
That was my favorite.

448
00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:26.880
I love that one, too.

449
00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:27.500
Mm-hmm.

450
00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:32.920
Now, during the office, there was no Dunkin' Donuts on the West CoaSt.

451
00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:40.840
And John Krasinski had this idea that he wanted to open up the first Dunkin' Donuts in L.A.

452
00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:42.360
Did he really?

453
00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:42.860
Yes.

454
00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:44.800
He was really into this for a while.

455
00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:48.180
And he tried to get Conan O'Brien to go in on it with them.

456
00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:48.960
Oh, my God.

457
00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:49.740
This was a whole thing.

458
00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:53.040
Could you imagine if Conan and John owned a Dunkin' Donuts?

459
00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:54.000
That was the plan.

460
00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:59.300
And I don't know why it fell apart or if this was just like a daydream.

461
00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:01.040
But he talked about it all the time.

462
00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:04.500
He wanted to open a Dunkin' Donuts franchise in L.A.

463
00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:05.120
And you know what?

464
00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:06.120
Now they're here.

465
00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:11.480
And whenever I pass by them, I wonder, is John, like, super pissed?

466
00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:13.480
Because he had that idea.

467
00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:15.400
That was John Krasinski's idea.

468
00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:16.620
To bring it to the West CoaSt.

469
00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:17.000
Yes.

470
00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.400
Well, that's all I got for Fast Facts, lady.

471
00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:22.440
Well, I found them delightful.

472
00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:23.620
And now I want a donut.

473
00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:24.460
Sorry.

474
00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:26.820
I really should have brought some Dunkin' Donuts in.

475
00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:27.440
Clearly.

476
00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:28.480
What was I thinking?

477
00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.100
I think we should go to break.

478
00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:31.740
And we'll come back and get into this episode.

479
00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:32.400
I love it.

480
00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:07.710
Listen to the We Do Not Care Club podcast with Melanie Sanders wherever you get your podcaSt.

481
00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:11.760
All right.

482
00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:12.800
We're back.

483
00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:18.560
This episode opens with Pam, Michael, and Ryan making paper deliveries.

484
00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:19.320
Yeah.

485
00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:25.060
And incidentally, I found out the working title for this episode was Delivery Day.

486
00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:25.820
Yes.

487
00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:27.660
Thank you, Randy, with those nuggets.

488
00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:28.920
Well, you know what?

489
00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:32.500
Michael starts the day at 4.30 in the morning with a prank.

490
00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:33.120
Mm-hmm.

491
00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:38.620
He pulls up to get Ryan, and then as Ryan gets in, he pulls up forward, and he pulls forward.

492
00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.860
And Ryan's just like, ugh, Jenna, that would drive me crazy.

493
00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:42.480
Yeah.

494
00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.780
I don't need people that peppy that early.

495
00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:45.400
No.

496
00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:46.560
That's too peppy.

497
00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:50.100
We got a fan question from Tyler B., who said this.

498
00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:57.840
My friend and I have watched the opening scene with Michael inching the delivery van forward while Ryan tries to get in over and over.

499
00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:05.820
At 46 seconds, we cannot figure out if Michael says, I lied or denied to Ryan.

500
00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:06.960
It's denied.

501
00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:14.560
He says, the captions say denied, but I'm not convinced because it almost sounds like delide.

502
00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:15.960
Do you know?

503
00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:17.600
Well, we have the script.

504
00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:18.700
What did it say?

505
00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:20.660
It was not a scripted moment.

506
00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:22.540
Oh, of course not.

507
00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:24.520
Of course they were playing around a little bit.

508
00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:28.500
I hear I lied, but I hear Steve's.

509
00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:33.700
Steve has this funny delivery he does sometimes where he, like, laughs at the beginning of a line.

